# One row per part that two composed layers are both built from, with the magnitude that would
# enter twice. The pairwise intersection of the fusions' part sets, carrying the shared part's
# own demand and nameplate: if anybody sums the two composed layers, this is the supply counted
# twice and this is how much of it there is.
#
# In the part's own unit, at its mode
#   The figures are the part's, in the part's unit. Each composer may convert the part by its own
#   factor, so in either composed layer's unit the doubled amount is this figure times that
#   composer's factor, and the two need not be equal. The mode is enough for what this relation
#   is: a referral that names the overlap, not a sum that owes a range.
#
# The exclusion is fully derivable here
#   This is the point of pivoting to `F`. A layer's quantity is a magnitude, and from |A| and |B|
#   nothing recovers |A intersect B|. But pivot from layer-indexed magnitudes to the part
#   incidence and the elements are named: the overlap of two composed layers is a part, a part is
#   a layer, and a layer carries its own figures. So `sum - e` closes with nothing filed. Set
#   theory reaches all the way.
#
# Why `asrt:Elimination` still has to exist
#   Inside one fusion the overlap need not be a whole part: `every-partial-elimination` shares 3 of
#   each part's 10, and `merge-holding-composition/compute` eliminates 90 against parts of 545 and
#   4.4. Nothing in the model names a slice of a part, so those magnitudes cannot be pivoted to and
#   must be observed. The sub-part overlap is the only unreachable case, and `e` is the element
#   that carries it.
#
# The two-root case beside the one-root rule
#   checks/jagged_layer.sqlc is the one-root case and this is the two-root case. That rule walks
#   (root, leaf) pairs inside a single descent, and scopes itself on purpose: "only a nested
#   composition can do this, so a single-level fusion is not examined however many parts it
#   names". A holding composing a group and naming a member directly is two paths from one root.
#   Two documents composing the same layer is two paths from two roots, no descent holds both, and
#   widening the leaf rule would change its quantifier rather than its reach.
#
# A referral, never a violation
#   Two filings composing the same layer is not a defect in either. It becomes one only if somebody
#   adds the two composed figures together, and whether anybody will is not a question a query can
#   answer. Third of a set that all sit on the boundary between filings: entries/spillovers.sqlc
#   finds two layers that move together with nobody saying so; the unjudged fungibility pairs find
#   two layers sharing a unit with nobody saying whether they are one layer; this finds two built
#   from the same supply.
#
# Pairs, not ordered pairs
#   The row comparison keeps it a set of pairs rather than a bag of ordered ones, admitting each
#   unordered pair once and dropping the reflexive case, so a composed layer is never reported
#   against itself.
-- composition/parts.sqlc crossed with itself on the part each side names, with that part's own figures.
SELECT a.part_filing, a.part_layer,
       a.composition    AS composition_a, a.composed_layer AS composed_a,
       b.composition    AS composition_b, b.composed_layer AS composed_b,
       (a.composition = b.composition) AS within_one_filing,
       f.d_mode         AS would_double_count_demand,
       f.n_mode         AS would_double_count_nameplate,
       f.d_unit         AS unit
FROM      (
    :compose(composition/parts.sqlc)
) a
JOIN      (
    :compose(composition/parts.sqlc)
) b ON  b.part_filing = a.part_filing
    AND b.part_layer  = a.part_layer
    AND (a.composition, a.composed_layer) < (b.composition, b.composed_layer)
LEFT JOIN (
    :compose(layers/figures.sqlc)
) f ON f.filing = a.part_filing AND f.layer = a.part_layer
